.TH SINDOOK-SEAL 1 "2026-08-18" "sindook 0.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sindook-seal \- encrypt files to recipients and/or a passphrase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sindook seal
[\fB\-r\fP \fIRECIPIENT\fP]... [\fB\-R\fP \fIFILE\fP]...
[\fB\-p\fP | \fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP]...
[\fB\-a\fP] [\fB\-o\fP \fIOUT\fP] [\fB\-f\fP] [\fIFILE\fP...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Seals files to recipients and/or a passphrase. Every recipient and
passphrase becomes a key slot; any one of them opens the file. Each
\fIFILE\fP becomes \fIFILE\fP\fB.sindook\fP; with no \fIFILE\fP,
stdin is sealed to stdout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-r\fP \fIRECIPIENT\fP
key file or literal \fBsindookpk1:\fP string, repeatable
.TP
\fB\-R\fP \fIFILE\fP
file with one public key per line, repeatable (blank lines and
\fB#\fP comments are skipped)
.TP
\fB\-p\fP
add a passphrase slot, prompted at the terminal
.TP
\fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read the passphrase from \fIFILE\fP instead, implies \fB\-p\fP
.TP
\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP
add files matched by a portable filesystem pattern, repeatable
.TP
\fB\-a\fP
armor: ASCII output that survives email and copy-paste
.TP
\fB\-o\fP \fIOUT\fP
output path, \fB\-\fP for stdout (single \fIFILE\fP only)
.TP
\fB\-f\fP
overwrite existing output
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sindook seal -r my.key.pub report.pdf
sindook seal -r alice.pub -r bob.pub -p budget.xlsx
sindook seal -R team.keys *.log
sindook seal -r alice.pub -glob "reports/*.pdf"
tar cz src | sindook seal -r my.key.pub -o src.tgz.sindook
sindook seal -r alice.pub -a -o - secret.txt | pbcopy
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sindook (1),
.BR sindook-open (1),
.BR sindook-rewrap (1),
.BR sindook-pubkey (1)
